[38;5;231m⏺[39m Buffer + manual export. That keeps the network out of it, but exported logs get pasted into tickets and chat, so
  redaction is still a real decision — just no longer a blocker.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether the module decides what's safe to log, or the call sites do. If the module enforces it, a careless
  [38;5;153mlogger.info("login", formData)[39m is safe by construction; if call sites do, the module is simpler but one bad call leaks
  a password into a buffer someone later exports.

  - [1mDenylist by key name[0m — the module redacts values for keys matching [38;5;153mpassword[39m, [38;5;153mtoken[39m, [38;5;153msecret[39m, [38;5;153mauth[39m, etc. when
    serializing. Buys: safe by default against the obvious mistakes, low friction. Costs: silently misses a sensitive
    field nobody named.
  - [1mAllowlist[0m — only explicitly-permitted fields get serialized; everything else becomes [38;5;153m[redacted][39m. Buys: no unnamed
    field can leak, ever. Costs: real friction — every new field you want to see needs registering, and the usual
    failure mode is a log line that says nothing.
  - [1mConvention only[0m — no automatic redaction; the rule lives in docs and review. Buys: simplest module. Costs: the
    guarantee is only as good as the next person in a hurry.

  Cheap to change afterward in all three cases — it's one function at the serialization boundary, not an interface.

  [1mMy recommendation: denylist by key name.[0m For a debugging logger the allowlist's friction reliably degrades into logs
  that omit the thing you needed, and the denylist catches every failure mode actually present in this code.

  Separately, and not part of this question: I'd drop the raw username from [38;5;153mapp.js:5[39m and log whether a username was
  [3mpresent[0m instead. A denylist keyed on "password" won't catch it, and it isn't what you need to debug a login failure.
